2.4 Key Findings from Literature

Finding 1 --- AI can automate crop disease detection

Deep-learning and computer-vision techniques provide effective
approaches for identifying diseases from crop images.

Finding 2 --- Machine learning can support agricultural recommendations

Existing research demonstrates the feasibility of recommending crops,
fertilizers, pesticides, and other agricultural inputs using data-driven
approaches.

Finding 3 --- Explainability can improve trust

XAI techniques can provide additional interpretation of AI-based disease
predictions.

Finding 4 --- Cloud-based agricultural systems require security

As agricultural applications increasingly rely on cloud infrastructure,
protecting transmitted and stored agricultural data becomes important.

Finding 5 --- PQC provides a future-oriented security direction

Post-quantum cryptography can be investigated to protect communication
against potential future quantum attacks.

Finding 6 --- Most Important

Existing research generally addresses disease detection, agrochemical
recommendation, and secure communication independently. This creates
an opportunity for an integrated framework such as AgroPQ.

III. Research Gap

3.1 Identified Research Gap

The major research gap identified from the literature is:

Limited integration of AI-based crop disease detection,
disease-aware agrochemical recommendation, and post-quantum secure
cloud communication within a single agricultural framework.

Existing research provides strong solutions for individual components,
but they are commonly developed independently.

3.2 Technical Limitations

Disease Detection

Existing disease-detection systems may face:

Dependence on training datasets

Limited crop/disease coverage

Variations in lighting and background

Differences between laboratory and field images

Generalization challenges

Agrochemical Recommendation

Existing recommendation systems may face:

Dependence on agricultural data quality

Limited integration with image-based disease diagnosis

Difficulty maintaining up-to-date agricultural knowledge

Need for reliable treatment information

Different agricultural conditions across regions

PQC Security

PQC-based communication may introduce:

Computational overhead

Communication overhead

Larger cryptographic artifacts depending on the algorithm

Implementation complexity

Key-management requirements

Therefore, AgroPQ must evaluate both security and performance.

3.3 Need for Proposed Work

A farmer may currently need separate processes:

Identify disease
       ↓
Consult expert / search treatment
       ↓
Find appropriate agrochemical
       ↓
Use separate application/cloud service
       ↓
Transmit/store data securely

AgroPQ aims to combine these steps into a single system:

              LEAF IMAGE
                  ↓
          AI DISEASE DETECTION
                  ↓
            DISEASE RESULT
                  ↓
       AGROCHEMICAL RECOMMENDATION
                  ↓
        SECURE CLOUD COMMUNICATION
                  ↓
              USER RESULT

This creates an end-to-end agricultural decision-support workflow.

